The recursive helper returns the node that should remain after processing the rest of the list. When the current node is the last one, it returns `nil`. Each earlier call reconnects its `Next` field to that result.

Dry run on `5 -> 8`: the call for `5` processes `8`, receives `nil`, and changes `5.Next` to `nil`. For an empty list or a single node, the public function also returns `nil`.

```go
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
type ListNode struct {
    Data int
    Next *ListNode
}
*/

func deleteTail(head *ListNode) *ListNode {
    if head == nil || head.Next == nil {
        return nil
    }
    return removeLast(head)
}

func removeLast(node *ListNode) *ListNode {
    if node.Next == nil {
        return nil
    }

    node.Next = removeLast(node.Next)
    return node
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` because each recursive call stays on the stack until the tail is reached.

Another simple way is to count the nodes first. The node before the tail is at position `length - 2`, so a second walk can reach it and remove the last link.

For example, with four nodes, the count is `4` and the pointer moves two times to reach the second node. Its `next` is then changed to `null`. The list is edited in place.

```java
class Solution {
    public ListNode deleteTail(ListNode head) {
        if (head == null || head.next == null) {
            return null;
        }

        int length = 0;
        ListNode current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            length++;
            current = current.next;
        }

        current = head;
        for (int i = 1; i < length - 1; i++) {
            current = current.next;
        }

        current.next = null;
        return head;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, with two passes that are still linear.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The last node cannot be removed directly unless we also know the node before it. This C++ version walks until `current->next->next` is the last node, then skips the last node.

For an empty list or a list with one node, the new head is `nullptr`. The input list is changed in place.

```cpp
class Solution {
    public:
        ListNode* deleteTail(ListNode* &head) {
            if (head == nullptr) {
                return nullptr;
            }

            if (head->next == nullptr) {
                head = nullptr;
                return head;
            }

            ListNode* current = head;
            while (current->next->next != nullptr) {
                current = current->next;
            }

            delete current->next;
            current->next = nullptr;
            return head;
        }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because the list may be scanned to its end.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

Recursion makes the stopping condition quite small here. The helper moves forward until it reaches the node whose `next` node is the tail. That link is then cleared.

A one-node list needs a separate check because there is no previous node. The method changes the original links and returns the possibly new head.

```java
class Solution {
    public ListNode deleteTail(ListNode head) {
        if (head == null || head.next == null) {
            return null;
        }

        deleteFromEnd(head);
        return head;
    }

    private void deleteFromEnd(ListNode node) {
        if (node.next.next == null) {
            node.next = null;
            return;
        }

        deleteFromEnd(node.next);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` because the recursive calls use the call stack.

## Intuition
Traverse till the second last node , so we keep checking  if next to next is null ie  we need to reach till second last node's ie second last nodes  next is null . 

## Approach

Check the edge cases if there is only one node or no node then we should return ;
Else traverse through the list until second last node , point the second last node to null so that its connection with last node is removed .. 

